# pgCowboy — project knowledge

Cross-platform **PostgreSQL role maintenance** desktop app: manage clusters, batch-run role operations via **user-defined PostgreSQL functions** (not direct DDL in app).

## Product decisions (do not regress)

| Topic | Decision |
|-------|----------|
| UI shell | **Wails v2** + embedded WebView; frontend is vanilla HTML/CSS/JS in `frontend/` |
| DB driver | **pgx/v5**; direct **TCP** to `host:port` (no SSH tunnels in v1) |
| Secrets | Optional per-cluster `connect_user` + `password` (both editable in the cluster editor; password is a masked field with a reveal toggle, stored plain-text in the private config written mode `0600`). Auth: user = cluster `connect_user` → `PGUSER` → **OS login user** (like psql); password = cluster `password` → `PGPASSWORD` → `~/.pgpass` → **empty** (trust, like psql). Production gated by a confirm popup (no checkbox). |
| Role logic | App calls **call templates** with `${placeholder}` → `SELECT fn($1,...)` — real DDL/privileges live in **DB functions** on each cluster |
| Categories | = cluster groups. Editable in Settings (label + base `color` + `confirm` flag); id is a slug of the label. Colours applied via a generated `<style id="cat-colors">` keyed on `data-cat` (no hardcoded prod/uat colours). `confirm` drives the production popup. |
| DB function specs | User will provide real names/signatures; defaults in `config.example.yaml` are placeholders (`your_schema.*`) |

## Stack (current)

- Go module: `github.com/michal-bartak/pgcowboy`
- Wails **v2.12.0** (CLI may auto-bump `go.mod` on `wails dev`)
- pgx v5, yaml.v3, google/uuid
- Bindings: `frontend/wailsjs/` — **auto-generated** by Wails; do not hand-edit; regenerate via `make dev` / `make build`
- **Always build through `make`, never a bare `wails dev`/`wails build`:** Wails' cgo asks pkg-config
  for `webkit2gtk-4.0`, so a 4.1-only host (Ubuntu 24.04+, Fedora) fails to compile. The Makefile
  detects it and adds `-tags webkit2_41` (`WAILS_BUILD_FLAGS`); every target that compiles Go must
  pass it, which is why `dev` is a target. Detected per host, not pinned in `wails.json`'s
  `build:tags` (committed, and 4.1 is wrong on a 4.0-only host / meaningless off Linux).

## Config files

Two files in one directory:

| OS | Directory |
|----|-----------|
| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/pgCowboy/` |
| Linux | `~/.config/pgcowboy/` |
| Windows | `%AppData%\pgCowboy\` |

`config.yaml` = app configuration; `clusters.yaml` = `categories`, `clusters`, `targets`. Example
templates: [`config.example.yaml`](config.example.yaml),
[`clusters.example.yaml`](clusters.example.yaml). Implemented in `internal/config/` (`paths.go`,
`store.go`); the split is the `yaml:"-"` on `model.Config`'s embedded `model.ClusterSet`.

**YAML keys:** `version`, `categories`/`clusters`/`targets` (clusters.yaml), `db_functions.<op>.call`, `db_functions.<op>.execution`, `batch.max_concurrency` (default 5), `ui.theme`, `ui.password_gen` (role-form random password generator: `length` clamped `[6,128]`/default 10, class toggles `lowercase/uppercase/digits/symbols`, `exclude_similar`; at least one class stays on — Settings → Password generator, persisted via `SaveUISettings`/`UISettings.PasswordGen`), `parent_roles` (preconfigured role parents — bare identifiers, offered as pick-list choices in the Assign-parents dialog, whose Role names field takes a comma-separated list; Create-role uses the same `parent_roles` placeholder as grant/revoke). Legacy `name`/`params` migrated on load.

## Wails API (`app.go` → `window.go.main.App`)

- `GetConfig`, `GetConfigPath`, `ReloadConfig`, `GetAppVersion`
- `AddCluster`, `UpdateCluster`, `DeleteCluster` (per-item; kept but unused by the UI now)
- `SaveClusters(ClustersConfig)` — staged Clusters editor: replaces the whole clusters+categories
  set atomically via `Store.SaveClustersAndCategories` (validate + UUID/slug ids + referential integrity)
- `SaveSettings(SettingsPayload)` — the whole Settings page in ONE atomic write (role parents,
  comment fields, search columns, db_functions, db_reads, batch, ui). Everything validates before
  anything is assigned, and templates are validated against the comment fields in the same payload.
  The per-section `SaveDBFunctions`/`SaveDBReads`/`SaveBatchSettings`/`SaveUISettings`/
  `SaveParentRoles`/`SaveCommentFields`/`SaveSearchColumns` remain but are unused by the UI.
- `GetDefaultTemplates` — built-in call templates + read queries for the editor's Default button,
  so `frontend/app.js` keeps no copy of the default SQL
- `SaveTargetSelection`
- `TestConnection`, `PreviewTargets`, `RunRoleBatch` (per-cluster transactional batch — the
  create/update/remove path, and the only write path; the legacy single-op `RunOperation`/
  `batch.Run`/`pg.CallFunction`/`commands.ValidateRequest` were deleted)
- **Introspection (reads, for Alter role):** `SearchRoles`, `LoadRoleDetails`,
  `LoadRoleDependencies` — scan the
  **selected** clusters concurrently (`internal/pg/introspect.go` + `batch.Runner`),
  collecting per-cluster errors instead of failing. The four read queries are **templatable**
  (`Config.DBReads`, YAML `db_reads.<search_roles|role_detail|role_parents|role_dependencies>.query`; vanilla
  catalog defaults + migrate/validate in `internal/config/dbreads.go`). `batch.Runner` passes
  each query into `pg` (which stays config-free); each takes one named `${rolename}` bind (rewritten
  to `$1` before Query; legacy `$1` still works) and its result columns
  are scanned **by name** via `pgx.RowToStructByNameLax` (order-independent; NULL comment/rolconfig
  OK; omitted contract column → zero; **extra** column → error). Contracts: search_roles →
  `rolname, comment`; role_detail → 7 `rol*` bools + `comment` + `rolconfig`; role_parents →
  `rolname`; role_dependencies → `database, dependency, class, object`. Editable in Settings →
  **Introspection queries** (`#fn-dialog` read mode; Default
  button reverts). Bound method `SaveDBReads`. Override a read with a privileged wrapper
  function/view that returns the contract columns.
- **`role_dependencies` is a pre-flight**, not a display read: BOTH removal paths (the red
  **Remove role** button and a pending presence removal published by **Save changes**) first run
  it on every targeted cluster and show `#deps-dialog`. A cluster with rows, or one whose
  check failed, defaults to **Skip** and is left out of the batch; a clean cluster is dropped
  without asking. The popup **replaces** the old `askConfirm('Remove role', …)`; production
  confirm still fires inside `executeRoleBatch`. Layout = **three sections** (`depsTier`: clean →
  could-not-be-checked → has-dependencies, ordered inside by group name then alias
  via `depsSortRows`): chips only / a `Cluster|Error|Skip-Try` table / per-cluster header + table.
  Clusters are labelled by ONE group-coloured `scopeLabelsHtml` chip (no alias text, no separate
  badge class — every pill in the app is the one `.chip` + `.chip-group`/`.chip-cluster`);
  all dependency tables are `table-layout:fixed` sharing one `depsColgroup` so columns line up. The
  `<h2>` holds `?` + **Reload** (`#deps-reload` → `reloadDeps`, re-reads the same clusters in place,
  keeping still-applicable Skip/Try picks via `mergeDepsChoices`) + ONE magnifier
  (`#deps-view-sql`), since every cluster runs the identical query (`LoadRoleDependencies` sets
  `Queries` on the error path too). A thrown read renders as all-clusters-failed
  (`depsErrorRowsFor`), never an `#ops-error` hidden behind the modal. Frontend: `preflightRemoval` /
  `loadDepsRows` / `initialDepsChoices` / `depsAllowedSet` / `filterSkippedRemovals` in
  `frontend/app.js`.

## Call templates (`internal/calltemplate/`)

Config: `db_functions.<operation>.call` + optional `.execution` (`function` \| `statement` \| `block`). Docs: [`sql/README.md`](sql/README.md).

| `operation` | Allowed `${...}` | Notes |
|-------------|------------------|-------|
| `create_role` | loginname, parent_roles, `${{<comment field>}}` (per `Config.CommentFields`) | `parent_roles`: statement → `"a", "b"`, function → inline `ARRAY['a','b']`; comment fields typed (string→literal, num/bool→bare, empty/absent→`NULL`); `ARRAY \|\|` requires `execution: function` |
| `remove_role` | loginname, rolename (alias) | `statement`: e.g. `DROP ROLE ${loginname}`; `block`: a complete anonymous block (`DO $tag$ … $tag$;`), run verbatim |
| `grant_parents` | loginname, parent_roles | `statement`: `GRANT ${parent_roles} TO ${loginname}` — identifiers, not quoted |
| `revoke_parents` | loginname, parent_roles | Default `statement`: `REVOKE ${parent_roles} FROM ${loginname}` |
| `change_password` | loginname, new_password | |
| `set_comment` | loginname, comment, `${{<comment field>}}` (per `Config.CommentFields`) | Default `statement`: `COMMENT ON ROLE ${loginname} IS ${comment}` — comment as escaped literal; comment fields typed (empty/absent→`NULL`) |
| `set_attribute` | loginname, attributes (alias: attribute) | Default `statement`: `ALTER ROLE ${loginname} WITH ${attributes}` — space-separated whitelisted keywords (SUPERUSER/NOSUPERUSER/…), embedded unquoted |
| `set_config` | loginname, config_name, config_value | Default `statement`: `ALTER ROLE ${loginname} SET ${config_name} = ${config_value}` — `config_name` is a bare unquoted GUC name (`fieldConfigName`, `gucNameRE`-validated), value a literal. Role GUCs read from `pg_roles.rolconfig`. |
| `reset_config` | loginname, config_name | Default `statement`: `ALTER ROLE ${loginname} RESET ${config_name}` |

**Execution:** `function` → `SELECT` + `$n` binds (`Query`). `statement` / `block` → embed values (`Exec`): role fields as identifiers (`loginname`, `parent_roles` list on grant_parents); passwords/names as quoted literals. Do not use raw `$1` in templates.

**Defaults are vanilla PostgreSQL DDL (statement mode)** — e.g. create_role default:

`CREATE ROLE ${loginname}`

(parent_roles and the configured comment-field placeholders are unused by the vanilla default; a
`function`-mode override can consume them, e.g.
`admin_access.create_role(${loginname}, NULL, ${{full_name}}, ${{e_mail}}, ARRAY['grp'] || ${parent_roles})`).
Loading an existing config never force-overwrites user templates; the `#fn-dialog` **Default**
button reverts one template to the vanilla built-in.

**Template processing:**

- **Function mode:** `${field}` → `$n` text bind from form (`internal/commands` arg map).
- **Two namespaces, never overlapping:** `${name}` = a built-in from the CLOSED per-op set; `${{name}}` = a configured comment field, always. One `tokenRE` matches both (inner classes exclude braces, so the bare branch can't swallow a double form); `parsedPlaceholder.ns` carries it and `placeholderKindFor` is the single source of truth for BOTH the SQL shape and the bound value — `buildFunctionQuery` previously took them from two disagreeing lookups, which corrupted `${comment}` on `set_comment` when a field was keyed `comment`. Comment-field values are namespaced in the args map via `model.CommentArgKey` (`cf:`) inside `commands.BuildArgs`; the Wails wire format is unchanged. Malformed `${…}` is rejected before substitution (`leftoverPlaceholder`). There is deliberately NO migration for the old single-brace form — the app is pre-release, so a stale template is simply rejected with a message naming the `${{…}}` form.
- **Statement/block:** `loginname`, `parent_roles`, `rolename` → double-quoted identifiers; `parent_roles` split on commas → `"a", "b"` (create_role/grant/revoke); `new_password`, `comment`, `config_value` → SQL string literals; **comment fields** (`${{key}}`, create_role/set_comment) → JSON-value-typed (string→literal, number/bool→bare, array/object→JSON literal, empty/`null`/absent→bare `NULL`), threaded in as variadic `commentFields ...string` from `Config.CommentFieldKeys()`.
- **Function mode `${parent_roles}`:** embedded inline as `ARRAY['a','b']` (verbatim single-quoted values via `renderRoleArrayVerbatim`; `'`-bearing values rejected; empty → `NULL`), NOT a `$n` bind — so wrapper functions receive a real `text[]`. The legacy `ARRAY[fixed] || ${parent_roles}` concat form still binds `$n::text[]` via `preprocessArrayOrNull`.
- `ARRAY['a','b'] || ${field}` → fixed array literal + `|| NULL` if field empty, else `|| $n::text[]` with `[]string{value}` (processed before other placeholders).
- `ARRAY[${field}, 'a', 'b']` → normalized to `ARRAY['a','b'] || ${field}` via `normalizeTemplate`.
- Reject `${Array...}`, `SELECT`, `;`. Legacy `${array_concat:...}` still parsed; migrate broken configs in `internal/config/dbfunctions.go`.

`internal/pg/function.go` → `calltemplate.Build` + `Query` (function) or `Exec` (statement/block).

## UI (`frontend/`)

Tabs: **Operations**, **Clusters**, **Settings**, with ONE right-aligned `.tab-actions` group per
page in the same bar — **Create role** + **Alter role** (`#op-tabs`) and **Add cluster** + **Test
connections** + **Cluster groups** (`#cluster-actions`); each declares its page via `data-for` and
the `.tab` handler shows only the active one, so a new group is markup only. The Clusters panel has
no in-panel toolbar. Prod/UAT
badges in CSS. Per-cluster outcomes render in the **Status** panel (hidden until
non-empty). Do not add a heavy frontend framework unless requested.

**UI density is one knob:** `html { font-size: 87.5% }` (= 14px) in `frontend/styles.css`. Every
content-sized value in the stylesheet is a `rem`, so that one declaration scales text, `--fs-*`,
`--control-h`/`--control-pad-*`, padding, gaps, table row heights and the `ch`-based grid tracks
together. Keep it that way: new CSS uses `rem` for anything content-sized, `px` only for hairline
borders (`1px`), radii and sub-10px decorations — a `px` width/`min-width` on a content box is the
bug, since it stays full-size while everything around it shrinks. **No icon carries a
`width`/`height`** (not in `index.html`, and `svgIcon`'s `size:` option was removed) — containers
size glyphs in rem. Hand-synced rem pairs that must not drift:
`th, td { padding: .55rem .75rem }` ↔ the `1.5rem` in `depsColgroup`; `.alter-result-head`'s
`calc(0.7rem + 1px)` ↔ the search-result row's border+padding.
Three tokens carry what rem cannot: **`--hairline: 1.15px`** for all borders (a flat `1px` is 1.75
device px at 175% scaling and snaps to 1 *or* 2 depending on position, so borders read patchy;
1.15px is always 2 there and still 1px at 100%) — decoration strokes stay literal px;
**`--ink-lift`** corrects optical centring (browsers centre the line box, so the cap band can land
off centre by `(fontBoxAscent - fontBoxDescent - capHeight) / 2` — measured 0.455px at `--fs-sm`
= the 0.042em token, but only 0.06px at `--fs-base`, so it goes on the small-font elements
(`button.small`, `.ph-chip`, `.pick-chip`) and **full-height buttons stay symmetric** like
`.tab`/`.seg-btn`; `min-height` is not a factor, and the removed `--ink-lift-control: 0.086em`
overcorrected buttons by ~1px — don't reintroduce it; uppercase pills excluded); `.chip` is the exception that proves it: the miscentring is per-font, so the chip is
centred structurally instead, by `text-box: trim-both cap alphabetic` behind `@supports` (the
padding edge becomes the ink edge, making `P` the padding itself and the snap exact; engines
without it keep the old centring, so the WebKit 16 / WebKitGTK 2.38 floor is unchanged).
**`--checkbox-lift`** for the Target-selection checkbox. Retune the token, never sweep the rules. **`.section-label`** is the one small-uppercase section label
(sized by `--fs-section`), replacing five drifted near-duplicates.

**Three WebKitGTK/Linux quirks, each fixed once.** (1) **`prefers-color-scheme` always reports
"light"** there, so *System* appearance is resolved by the backend: `App.IsSystemDark()`
(`system_theme_linux.go`; `!linux` stub in `system_theme_other.go`) probes GNOME `gsettings`
(`color-scheme`, then pre-42 `gtk-theme`) then KDE (`kreadconfig6`/`5`), each returning
`(dark, known)` so "no gsettings" is unknown, not light. Only Linux calls it (`onLinux()` caches
`runtime.Environment().platform`); elsewhere `matchMedia` still answers. `applyTheme` is therefore
**async**, guarded by a **generation counter** (`themeGeneration`, re-checked after each `await`) so
a superseded probe can't repaint, and Linux gets a **5 s poll** (`systemThemePoll`) because the
media query never fires `change` for a switch the engine can't see. (2) **A `<select>` is two problems.** The closed control needs both
`appearance: none` and `-webkit-appearance: none` (WebKitGTK honours only the prefixed one, so
without it Linux draws the native GTK combo). The **drop-down list is native and NO CSS reaches
it** — `select option {…}` is kept for the platforms where it works but does NOT fix Linux; the
only knob is GTK's `gtk-application-prefer-dark-theme` via `App.SetNativeDarkTheme(bool)`
(`native_theme_linux.go`, cgo/`gtk+-3.0`; `g_idle_add` defers the call onto the GTK main loop).
Its constraint **`linux && cgo && (dev || production)`** is load-bearing — Wails gates its own cgo
the same way, so the bare `go build`/`go test` CI commands need no GTK headers; `native_theme_other.go`
holds the exact complement. Those bare commands compile none of the cgo, so the first thing that
type-checks it is `release.yml`'s Linux build job, or a local `make build` on Linux — build there
before tagging a change to it. `applyTheme`'s `paint()` drives page
and GTK together, poll tick included.
(3) **Wails sets no GTK window icon** — `main.go` embeds `build/appicon.png` as `linux.Options.Icon`
with `ProgramName: "pgcowboy"` (`g_set_prgname()` = Wayland `app_id` / X11 `res_name`; must equal
the `pgcowboy.desktop` basename, and defaults to the executable name `pgCowboy` otherwise), and
`StartupWMClass` is kept in step. Passing a non-nil `linux.Options` means **`WebviewGpuPolicy` must
be set explicitly to `Never`** — Wails applies that default (wailsapp/wails#2977) only when the
struct is nil.

**Scrollbars: `::-webkit-scrollbar` only.** The standard `scrollbar-width`/`scrollbar-color` sit
inside a `@supports not selector(::-webkit-scrollbar)` guard (Firefox smoke-test path) and must not
be re-added outside it: set to anything but `auto`, Chromium 121+ and WebKit/WebKitGTK ignore every
`::-webkit-scrollbar` rule on the element, which restores the platform **overlay** bar — on
WebKitGTK a hair-thin self-hiding indicator that paints over content. An explicit
`::-webkit-scrollbar` width is also what force-renders an overlay bar as a classic, space-taking
one. **One grouped `overflow-y: scroll` rule** (`.ops-body, .settings-body, .cluster-list,
.table-scroll, .run-queries-content, .deps-list, .scope-targets, #comments-list,
.search-dialog-body .alter-results, textarea`) reserves the bar's lane so content never shifts when
it appears; it OWNS the vertical axis (no container declares its own `overflow-y`; each keeps only
`overflow-x`), and it is not `scrollbar-gutter: stable`, which is above the WebKit 16 / macOS 12
floor. Nothing paints while a container cannot scroll, so an unused lane is invisible.

**The bar rides in the right margin; content keeps equal left/right margins.** Tokens:
**`--page-pad`** (1.4rem — the app's horizontal edge, read by `.app-header`, `.tabs`, `.ops-layout`,
both panels and `dialog > div/form`, so content, action buttons and op-tabs share one vertical line),
**`--scrollbar-w`** (8px, repeated as a literal in the `::-webkit-scrollbar` rule on purpose) and
**`--scrollbar-gap`** = `calc((var(--page-pad) - var(--scrollbar-w)) / 2)` — derived, not picked, so the
bar sits centred in the margin (equal air to the content/frame line and to the window/dialog edge) and
the `bar + gap ≤ --page-pad` invariant holds by construction. A second
grouped rule gives the borderless scrollers `margin-right: calc(-1 * (var(--scrollbar-w) +
var(--scrollbar-gap)))` + `padding-right: var(--scrollbar-gap)`, so their content edge lands back on
the panel's content edge and the bar sits in the margin. Don't give any of them a `padding`/`margin`
shorthand later in the sheet (it drops the longhands — hence `.ops-body`'s `padding-block`) and don't
give an ancestor an `overflow` value (`.ops-main` / `.table-wrap` are deliberately `overflow:
visible`). `.ops-sidebar` is the exception: bordered panel, keeps `overflow-y: auto`, and hands the
right margin to the nested `.cluster-list`, which uses the same formula — its padding is `--page-pad`
(not a bespoke 1rem) so the rows clear the frame by the window margin and the 15px of bar+gap fits
with air left over instead of the bar touching the frame.

**Split tables.** `.table-wrap` = bordered, NON-scrolling flex column of `.table-head-clip >
table.table-head` (thead) + `.table-scroll > table` (tbody): headers stay visible, the bar sits
outside the border in the panel margin, its track spans the data rows only, the box border never
moves. `.table-scroll` grows past a BORDER (not out of a padding box), so its margin/padding pair adds
`--hairline` — otherwise the frame line eats part of the gap and the bar reads as centred in the margin
instead of centred like the other scrollers. `overflow: visible` is load-bearing (hence the corner radii on `.table-wrap thead th`). Both
tables share ONE generated colgroup under `table-layout: fixed` (`tableColgroup` /
`applyTableColumns` / `tableMinWidth` in app.js — character counts, like `depsColgroup`, never text
measuring), plus a `scrollLeft` sync so the header follows sideways scrolling. Values can be clipped,
so `.table-scroll td` / `.table-head th` ellipsize and the renderers set `title` on truncatable
cells.

**App-shell layout:** `body` is a flex column with `overflow:hidden` (no whole-page
scroll); header + tabs fixed. Operations = two independently scrolling columns
(`.ops-sidebar` / `.ops-main`); `.ops-main` has a scrolling `.ops-body` + pinned
`.ops-footer` (Run or Save/Remove, via `updateOpsFooter()`). Clusters/Settings are
single-column, sharing a padded panel + inset **`[Discard] [Save]`** footer (Settings:
`btn-discard-settings`/`btn-save-settings`+`discardSettings`; the divider goes up to the content
margins, not the window). The **Clusters tab is STAGED**
like Settings: cluster/group add/edit/delete mutate `clustersDraft`/`categoriesDraft` (seeded
once from saved `state` in `loadConfig`); nothing persists until the pinned **Save**/**Discard**
footer (`btn-save-clusters` → `App.SaveClusters` → `Store.SaveClustersAndCategories`, atomic
validate+replace; Save enabled-when-dirty / disabled-when-clean via `clustersDirty`). Only the Clusters editor
reads the drafts — Operations keeps reading saved `state`. **Test connections**
(`btn-test-clusters` → `testAllClusters`) tests the on-screen **draft** values via
`TestConnectionInput`; the per-row **Status** column carries a one-word `OK`/`Error`/`testing…`
label (`setClusterStatus`, full message in the `title`) and the detail goes to the Clusters footer's
own status chip (`#cluster-test-status`, state `clusterTestState`, seeded from the DRAFT rows via
`buildStatusState(rows,'queued')` and patched per result; `restoreClusterStatuses` repaints the
cells `renderClustersTable` rebuilds). Left sidebar is Target selection only; "Or pick clusters" collapses/expands
(collapsed by default; expanded list flexes to fill). Target selection is **remembered**
(module-level `selectedCategoryIds`=null→all / `selectedClusterIds`, seeded once from
persisted `Config.Targets`, re-render keeps it, persisted via `SaveTargetSelection`) so it
survives Settings-save and restarts. **Cluster groups** are edited from
the tabs bar's Clusters actions (`btn-manage-groups` → `#groups-dialog` list →
`#group-dialog` form; no Save button, so not in Settings). Feature descriptions live
behind a **`?` help badge** (`.q-hint`, via `hintBadge(text)`); mouse hover shows a shared
`position:fixed`, viewport-clamped popover (`.q-hint-pop`) driven by delegated
`mouseover`/`keydown` handlers (no forced `tabindex`; opens on Enter/Space when
keyboard-focused, not on plain focus). Its optical nudge rides on `vertical-align: 0.07em`, never a
`position/top`: flex containers centre the badge themselves and ignore `vertical-align`, so the
correction self-scopes to inline labels — a `top` applied to both and left every flex label ~1.1px
high.

**Shared UI conventions (solve once, don't re-patch per popup):** open every `<dialog>` via
the `openModal(dlgOrId)` helper (never bare `.showModal()`) — it drops `showModal()`'s
auto-focus ring on the first Close/OK button unless the dialog has an `[autofocus]` element — and
close every one via `closeModal(dlgOrId)` (never bare `.close()`): closing synchronously restores
focus to the opener and the engine rings it even for a mouse-driven open, so `closeModal` blurs it
when `lastInputWasPointer` (capture-phase `pointerdown`/`keydown`). Keyboard/Esc keeps the ring. It
relies on neither the `close` event nor a focus event — some engines fire neither.
Focus indicators are keyboard-only (`:focus-visible`) and **inset** (border + `inset`
box-shadow; light ring on primary-filled controls) so scroll/`overflow:hidden` containers
can't clip them — one `styles.css` rule for text fields/buttons/checkboxes. Base `dialog` is
`padding:0`; each `*-dialog-body` sets its own padding. Header `.brand` row (dot + smaller
title + version chip + `ⓘ #btn-about`) opens `#about-dialog` (`GetAppVersion`;
`runtime.BrowserOpenURL` links).

**Alter role** (search-then-edit): clicking the **Alter role** op-tab opens a search
dialog (no separate "Find user" button) that searches the *selected*
clusters (name + `COMMENT ON ROLE`), grouped by login; picking one opens a single
consolidated form. Role Parents (`pg_auth_members`; labelled "Role Parents", never "Privileges" —
the *Assign parents…* button opens the scope dialog, whose *Role names* field takes a
comma-separated list via `parseRoleNameList`) and attributes (superuser, createrole,
createdb, inherit, login, replication, bypassrls) list one-per-row with **scope labels**
judged per group (outlined/bordered group label when all selected clusters of a group match
— matching Target selection's group boxes — else filled per-cluster labels; coloured by
environment). **✎ Edit** opens a per-cluster
checkbox editor that grants **and** revokes; **×** removes everywhere; **↺** discards
pending. **Settings** (role GUCs) use the same editor keyed by `name=value`
(`alterConfigSet`/`alterConfigReset`), emitting `set_config`/`reset_config`. Comments
popup groups clusters by comment content (compared as JSON by value) and saves via
`set_comment`. Edit state: `alterAdd`/`alterRevoke`, `alterAttrAdd`/`alterAttrRemove` are
`Map<key, Set<clusterId>>`; `describeScope` / `scopeLabelsHtml` produce all labels.

**Create and Alter share one `#role-form`** rendered by `renderAlterDetail` over
`alterDetails` + the `alter*` edit maps. Mode = `isCreateMode()` (`currentOp`). Create =
editing a not-yet-existing role with an empty synthetic baseline (`synthCreateBaseline()`
from `resolveSelectedClusters()`; `reconcilePendingWithUniverse()` on selection change);
Alter = search → `pickUser` → `reloadDetails`. The static `#role-identity` block holds login
(readonly in edit) + an inline **comment editor** (`#role-comment-editor`, module-level
`commentEditor` state, `loadCommentEditor`). Comments are **format-agnostic** (plain text or
arbitrary JSON, no forced keys): Fields↔Raw toggle, Fields edits string values only, labels
come from Settings-managed `Config.CommentFields` (`comment_fields`, **empty by default and never
refilled on load**, `SaveCommentFields`); the mode always follows `ui.comment_default_view`
(`preferredCommentView`, default **raw**) except for plain text, which has no fields and stays Raw;
`editorFromComment`/`assembleComment(From)`/`switchEditorMode`/`parseCommentObject` are the core
helpers. **Find-role result columns are configured too**, never a hardcoded comment key:
`Config.SearchColumns` (`search_columns`, ordered `{label,template}`, `SaveSearchColumns`, Settings
→ **Role Details** list editor / `searchColumnsDraft`) is rendered frontend-side by
`renderSearchTemplate` over `RoleMatch.Comment` — same two namespaces as the call templates:
`${{<key>}}` = any JSON comment key, and the closed bare set's only member `${comment}` =
the raw comment, unresolved → `''`, result whitespace-collapsed; `searchCellValues` returns one string
per column (first non-empty in `byGroupThenAlias` order) and does **not** flag clusters disagreeing —
that is reported after the role loads, by the "Comments differ" banner and the Comments dialog. Layout: **CSS subgrid**. The container `#alter-results` owns the tracks
(`--search-cols` from `searchGridTemplate(colCount)`: `fit-content(40ch)` rolename, one
`minmax(4ch, max-content)` per column, `minmax(8ch, auto)` chips — a column's max is neither a
`fit-content` cap (which ellipsized long values while space sat unused) nor `auto` (which stretched
every column past its content and pushed the next one ~240px away); the chips track is the only
flexible one, and being last + `justify-self: end` it holds the slack and stays flush right); the header and every row are
`grid-column: 1 / -1` + `grid-template-columns: subgrid`, so the browser does the sizing. A subgrid
child's border+padding insets its tracks, so `.alter-result-head` must keep
`padding: 0 calc(0.7rem + 1px)` to match the row; the column gap lives on the container and is
inherited. This needs **WebKit 16 / macOS 12+ / WebKitGTK 2.38+** — see the installation docs.
`search_columns` has
no `omitempty` so an explicit `[]` ("rolename only") survives a restart while an absent key gets the
default; `searchColumns()` therefore has no frontend default fallback. `pg.ParseFullName` and the
`FullName` fields are gone — don't reintroduce them. Run: both modes build per-cluster ordered op
lists and send ONE
`App.RunRoleBatch(RoleBatchRequest)` (`executeRoleBatch`); the backend (`batch.runClusterTx`) runs
each cluster's ops in a **single transaction** (commit/rollback, one result per cluster, bounded
concurrency), emitting a `role-batch-progress` Wails event (`model.ClusterProgress`) per cluster
start/finish so the UI shows live progress in the footer status chip `#run-status` (button-sized,
neutral color, spinner-only-while-running, no ✓/✕ glyph) (+ its `#run-status-dialog` popup — no
Host column; each done row has a magnifier (`.rst-view`) opening a larger `#run-queries-dialog`
with that cluster's executed SQL — not shown inline — plus a copy button that copies message +
all queries). Each `ClusterResult`/`ClusterProgress` carries `Queries []string` (executed SQL from
`pg.ExecuteOperation`'s `(sql, msg, err)` return; function-mode binds inlined; failing op's SQL
kept). `clearRunStatus()` clears it on op-tab/page-tab switch so it never leaks across pages.
**No corner toast** (`showToast` removed): simple confirmations flash the action button
("Saved"/"Created", `flashButton`); Save buttons are enabled-when-dirty / disabled-when-clean,
no marker (`setDirty` toggles `disabled`; disabled = `pointer-events:none`, no hover — Settings
`settingsDirty`, role `buildAlterClusterOps().length>0`, Clusters `clustersDirty`). All
right-aligned action buttons + op-tabs align to the 1.25rem panel edge. Errors/validation render
inline in a `.form-error` (`showInlineError` →
`#ops-error`/`#settings-error`/`#clusters-error`/`#scope-error`/`#group-error`/
`#cluster-test-error`/`#alter-search-errors`) + a red button flash; run outcomes stay in the chip.
The **Find-role popup** has its OWN status chip (`#search-status`, bottom-left of its `<menu>`) fed
by `searchState` = `buildStatusState(SearchRoles rows)` — independent of `runState` and of the
Clusters page's `clusterTestState` (a search runs
only `SearchRoles`; the detail load comes later via `pickUser`→`reloadDetails` and owns the footer
chip). Clicking it opens the shared `#run-status-dialog` via `openRunStatusDialog(rs)`, which sets
`statusDialogState`; `runStatusSummary(rs)`/`renderRunStatusDialog(rs)` take the state explicitly.
The status table's rows are ordered by **group name then alias** for every usage
(`statusRowOrder` → the shared `byGroupThenAlias`, also used by `depsSortRows` and matching the
Clusters table), never by arrival order and never by status; a group that no longer exists sorts
last (`groupRank`). `categoryOrderIndex` (configured order) is now only `describeScope`'s
chip order.
Search failures are a **one-liner** (`searchFailureLine`) — `renderAlterErrors`' per-cluster list is
gone, and `stripClusterPrefix` removes the `connect to <alias>: ` that `pg.Connect` adds when a
Cluster column already names it. `pg.cleanConnectError` dedupes pgx's interleaved TLS+plaintext
dial attempts ("A B A B" → "A B").
`buildAlterClusterOps` orders grant→revoke→password→**set_attribute (all keywords
combined)**→set_config→reset_config→set_comment; create prepends `create_role`
(`buildCreateClusterOps`). Comment persists via `set_comment` (per-cluster `commentOverrides` wins,
else `assembleComment`; create's/presence-add's `create_role` gets its comment-field placeholders
from `commentFieldArgs(effectiveComment)`, follow-up `set_comment` authoritative). When comments vary the inline editor hides and the Comments
dialog reconciles per cluster (`commentVersionEditors`). `.rce-fields` (both surfaces) is a
`repeat(auto-fill, minmax(14rem, 1fr))` grid — `auto-fill` on purpose, or two fields would stretch
across the form — and `.role-identity` is deliberately uncapped so the column count can follow the
window; the login input carries its own 13.125rem cap. One shared
`confirmScopeDialog`/`buildScopeTargets` (no create fork). **Errors never touch the role form**:
`executeRoleBatch` returns the results array or `null`; on `null`/any per-cluster failure the
Save/Create/Remove paths leave the form + pending edits intact (chip/popup are the only error
surface). Only a fully clean run refreshes the form — Save re-reads via `fetchRoleDetails` and
re-renders only when that reload is itself clean (never clobbers on transient unreachable); the
"not found" empty state still renders for a real search `pickUser`.

## Versioning & packaging

- App semver: [`VERSION`](VERSION) — git tags `v$(cat VERSION)` (see [`RELEASING.md`](RELEASING.md)).
  The **only** place a version is maintained by hand: `main.go` embeds it (no ldflags needed), the
  installer scripts read it for artifact names + the MSI `ProductVersion`. Don't restate a version
  number in prose/docs — use a `{VERSION}` placeholder.
- `wails.json` `info.productVersion` is a **generated mirror** — never hand-edit it.
  `make sync-wails-version` owns it (writes only on a real change, printing
  `… (updated - commit this)`), and `build`/`build-ci` depend on it, so every release package has
  matching bundle metadata. It exists only because Wails v2 hardcodes its config path and has no
  flag/env for the version; dropping the key defaults it to a hardcoded `1.0.0`.
- `make package` — `wails build` with `-ldflags` (version/commit/date) + the host OS's **native
  installer** in `dist/`: `.dmg` (macOS), `.msi` (Windows, WiX v3 from
  `build/windows/installer/product.wxs`), `.deb` + `.rpm` (Linux, fpm). No archives.
- One script per platform in `build/scripts/`; `.github/workflows/release.yml` runs the same
  scripts through `make package-ci`, so fix packaging in the script, not the YAML.
  `PLATFORM=darwin/universal` builds the universal macOS bundle. Never change the MSI
  `UpgradeCode` GUID.
- UI: `GetAppVersion()`; config YAML `version:` is **schema** version only.
- [`CHANGELOG.md`](CHANGELOG.md) (Keep a Changelog) is the user-facing history. Add user-facing
  changes under `## [Unreleased]` **as part of the change** — one terse line each (bold feature
  name + what it does, no narration), phrased for a user, not a diff reader.
  At release time `[Unreleased]` becomes `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD`; the heading must match `VERSION`
  because `release.yml` extracts that section (awk, up to the next `## [`) into the release body,
  above the install notes and GitHub's auto-generated commit list, plus a link to the whole file.

